'''Jujube''' (UK {{IPAc-en|ˈ|dʒ|uː|dʒ|uː|b}}; US {{IPAc-en|ˈ|dʒ|u|dʒ|u|b}} or {{IPAc-en|ˈ|dʒ|u|dʒ|@|b|iː}};<ref name=":0">{{Cite OED|jujube }}</ref> scientific name '''''Ziziphus jujuba'''''), '''jujuba''', '''Chinese jujube''',<ref name="AH">{{cite journal |last1=Liu |first1=M.J. |last2=Zhao |first2=Z.H. |last3=Qi |first3=Y.F. |date=August 2009 |title=International Survey and Discussion on the Names of Genus Jujube and Its Main Species |url=https://www.actahort.org/books/840/840_9.htm |journal=Acta Horticulturae  |issue=840 |pages=81–88 |doi=10.17660/ActaHortic.2009.840.9|url-access=subscription }}</ref>{{rp|82}} '''red date''' or '''Chinese date''' is a species in the genus ''[[Ziziphus]]'' in the buckthorn family [[Rhamnaceae]].<ref name="POWO_719213-1"/> It is often confused with the closely related Indian jujube, ''[[Ziziphus mauritiana|Z.{{nbsp}}mauritiana]]''. The jujube tolerates a diverse range of climates, from [[Temperate climate|temperate]] to [[tropical climate|tropical]]. Its origin is thought to be in eastern Asia, but it has been widely dispersed through cultivation, and is today cultivated in gardens as a shrub as well as in agriculture as a food crop. Its fruit is eaten freshly harvested as well as [[dried fruit|dried]] and candied.

== Names ==
The name ''jujuba'' is directly [[Latin]]<ref name="AH"/>{{rp|82}} while ''jujube'' through a [[Romance languages|Romance language]] (likely French ''jujube''),<ref name=":0" /> both derive from [[Classical Latin|Classical]] {{lang|la|zizyphum}} (used for the fruit) and {{lang|la|zizyphus}} (the tree) from [[Ancient Greek language|Ancient Greek]] {{lang|grc|ζίζυφον}} ''zízyphon''.<ref>{{Cite web |first1=Henry George |last1=Liddell |first2=Robert |last2=Scott |title=A Greek-English Lexicon, ζίζυ^φον |url=https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0057:entry=zi/zufon |access-date=13 February 2023 |website=www.perseus.tufts.edu }}</ref> The name ''jujube'' is not connected to botanically unrelated [[jojoba]] (''Simmondsia chinensis'') hispanicized from native [[Oʼodham language|Oʼodham]] ''hohohwi''.<ref>{{Cite OED|jojoba }}</ref>

Jujube [[#Description|fruit]]s are also known as "red dates" or "Chinese dates" by resemblance to [[date palm#Description|date fruits]] of the ''[[date palm|Phoenix dactylifera]]'' palm.<ref name="AH"/>{{rp|82}}

==Description==
It is a small [[deciduous tree]] or [[shrub]] reaching a height of {{convert|5|–|10|m|abbr=off}}, usually with thorny branches. The [[leaves]] are shiny-green, ovate-acute, {{convert|2|–|7|cm|abbr=off|frac=4}} long and {{cvt|1|–|3|cm|frac=8}} wide, with three conspicuous veins at the base, and a finely toothed margin.<ref name=rushforth>{{cite book |last=Rushforth |first= K. |year=1999 |title=Trees of Britain and Europe |publisher=Collins |isbn=0-00-220013-9 |pages=1271–1272}}</ref> Leaves of trees grown in the climate region in Turkey measure average between 3.8–4.28 cm in length and 1.79–1.98 cm in width.<ref>{{cite conference |author1=Melekber Sulusoglu |author2=Aysun Cavusoglu  |author3=Nur Dede |author4=Hülya Unver |date=16–21 February 2014  |title=Morphological, pomological and nutritional traits of jujube (''Zizyphus jujuba'' Mill.) |url=https://www.cabidigitallibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.5555/20163271326  |conference=49. hrvatski i 9. međunarodni simpozij agronoma |location=Dubrovnik, Hrvatska |publisher=Zbornik Radova }}</ref> The [[flower]]s are small, {{convert|5|mm|frac=8}} wide, with five inconspicuous yellowish-green petals.

